a nearly two-hour long phone call  between u.s president joe biden and xi  jinping has ended the leaders of the  world's two biggest economies spoke for  the first time since november washington  wants to stop beijing from providing  military and economic help to vladimir  putin chinese state media reports she  has told biden that conflict is not in  anyone's interest for more on this let's  head to  the white house where our correspondent  kimberly hellcat is standing by  kimberly first time these two leaders  have spoken since the war in ukraine  began what are the two men trying to  achieve and are there any hints of any  progress  well what we know is from the u.s side  the white house press secretary was very  clear about what the united states and  the west was hoping to achieve and that  was to really pin down from a u.s  standpoint from the western standpoint  where exactly china stands with regard  to its relationship with russia in other  words there has been grave concern about  the in the words of the white house  press secretary jinsaki the absence of  denunciation by china with regard to the  russian invasion of ukraine and  according to her she believes that  speaks volumes around the world and as a  result there's also been a lot of  concern about the alignment between  china and russia  specifically reports that china has  potentially been selling weapons to  russia to assist  in the ground fighting as well as in the  air and so this has been something that  the u.s president was hoping to try and  secure in terms of  where this relationship is moving  forward where the united states can  count on china and whether or not the  united states and the west can count on  china to use its economic leverage with  russia to potentially secure a ceasefire  in this conflict so this is the sort of  essence of the call we know that it  lasted nearly two hours the u.s  president took it in the situation room  of the white house that is a secure  location  and that this comes on the heels of a  discussion that took place earlier in  the week between the national security  adviser jake sullivan and his  counterpart in rome that conversation  lasting about seven hours so you can see  there was a lot to discuss and again a  lot of concern about where china stands  and whether or not it will assist in  trying to resolve this crisis  and kimberly as you mentioned sort of  it's no secret that this relationship  has been strained at the best of times  talk us through the relationship between  she and uh bro and biden  well the u.s president has said that he  believes that  chinese president xi is his friend but  when it comes to  their  sort of ideological perspectives they're  very different  the us president spoke about this in  fact as he was speaking at a luncheon on  capitol hill just on thursday he said  that chinese president xi  believes that there is no future for  democracy and the u.s president believes  just the opposite he believes that this  is an inflection point in history in  other words that the battle right now  globally is between autocracies  and democracies the u.s president  believing democracies will prevail and  that is not a view shared by chinese  president xi and so this is where the  battle of ideologies comes in and it  really transcends everything whether  it's the military whether it's the  economy uh whether it is the social  structure of these two nations and this  is why their differences have been at  times so difficult to resolve and we've  seen this play out for years  not just when it comes to the economic  relationship the sanctions that have  been levied against one another and  their economies but also over the issue  of taiwan and the concerns that the  united states has there and some of the  actions that the that china has taken uh  militarily and and sort of asserted  itself in the south china sea so uh  these are things that the united states  and the west are watching very closely  they're watching closely as china exerts  its pressure on russia because of course  this could have another chapter in in  its own region that would make the  united states very nervous so this is  why the united states is trying to keep  this dialogue open the press secretary  believes that there is the opportunity  to resolve some of these different  differences and at the same time have a  potential ceasefire in this imminent  crisis oh to be a fly on the wall during  that conversation kimberly thank you  very much for breaking it down for us  kimberly hellcat live for us in  washington dc
 

